Rebase the timer CALIBRATION functions FUN_1000_2fd3 + FUN_1000_3064 from the wrong SS static
context (0x2ba9) to the real runtime segment (DS=SS=DGROUP=0x1c00). Class: dynamic-DS-from-SS
(docs/stage1.md; same family as patch 006's ss:[0x70] and patch 013's dynamic-DS).

ROOT CAUSE (asm-verified, base-0 0x12fd3.. and 0x13064..): both functions begin `mov ax,ss; mov ds,ax`
so DS = SS for the rest of the body. At RUNTIME the engine runs in the DOS small model with
SS=DS=DGROUP=0x1c00, so those `ds:[0xNN]` accesses target DGROUP (linear 0x1c000+NN). But Ghidra's
PrepAnalysis SS context is the load-time placeholder 0x2ba9, so it based every DS-after-mov-ds-ss
access at 0x2ba90 -> the calibration RESULTS were written to a dead data region (DAT_2000_bXXX/dXXX,
linear 0x2bXXX) instead of the DGROUP timer block. Net effect: DAT_1000_d8b8 (the ISR's per-tick
fractional STEP) and DAT_1000_d8c6 (the PIT divisor) stayed 0, so the INT-8 ISR's accumulator
`d8b6 += d8b8` never carried and DGROUP:0x452 (the tick counter FUN_1000_3346 busy-waits on) never
advanced -> boot hang. The ISR (FUN_1000_30f8/31c3) and the spin caller (FUN_1000_2ebe) already read
these as the correct DGROUP DAT_1000_ symbols, so the writers were simply mis-based.

FIX: rename each DS=SS-misbased symbol to its DGROUP counterpart (linear - 0x2ba90 + 0x1c000):
  badc->c04c(ss:0x4c vector) d344->d8b4 bedc->c44c d354->d8c4 d356->d8c6(PIT divisor)
  d348->d8b8(STEP) d34c->d8bc d350->d8c0 d352->d8c2(2f71's calib) d35e->d8ce
All references to these symbols are confined to these two functions; FUN_1000_2f71 already writes
d8c2 with the correct DGROUP base (default DS ctx), so after this the calib chain is consistent:
2f71 -> d8c2; 3064 reads d8c2, writes d8b8/d8bc/d8c0/d8ce; 2fd3 writes c44c/d8c4/d8c6 -> ISR ticks.
